    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm having some trouble with my sourdough starter stability lately. I've noticed some really erratic pH swings in my levain—sometimes it's way too acidic too fast, and it's messing with my dough's extensibility and final rise. I'm currently using a 20% rye inoculation with a high-protein bread flour, but the microbial activity seems all over the place. Can you explain how ambient temperature fluctuations and the specific protein content of my flour might be interacting to cause these sudden shifts in the Lactobacillus vs. yeast balance?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Temperature fluctuations directly affect microbial metabolism, making warmer conditions encourage faster, sometimes more aggressive lactic acid bacteria (LAB) activity, leading to rapid $\\text{pH}$ drops. Higher protein content in your bread flour provides more amino acids, acting as a readily available food source for both yeast and LAB. When combined, warm temperatures might accelerate the LAB's ability to consume these proteins and produce lactic or acetic acid quickly, overwhelming the yeast's slower acid production and causing those erratic drops.",
