      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It's likely the *rate of change* rather than a single threshold that causes instability in the sourdough community. Microbes thrive in a narrow optimum range; even small, rapid shifts outside this optimal temperature trigger metabolic stress, forcing the bacterial populations to dramatically adjust their fermentation pathways. When the rate of change is rapid, the LAB can suddenly ramp up acid production disproportionately to the yeast's growth, leading to those sudden $\\text{pH}$ spikes.",
      "source": "target",
